Subject: [PATCH 10277/11362] Resolve the 43 held-back fabrication stamps per
 artifact, and correct two I mis-targeted

The operator's instruction was to refactor these rather than sweep them, and the
distinction turned out to be load-bearing. Re-judged with the live gate, ALL 43
draw no critical flag -- which is exactly what a sweep would rest on, and it is
not sufficient. Three of the nine "no reason" stamps have substantive findings
that live in COMMIT MESSAGES, where no consumer of the fabrication gate reads
them:

  exp3929 -- action_efficiency_ratio=1.959 is CIRCULAR. task_potential() reads
    oracle goal fields the synthetic env exposes by construction, then plants a
    fixed string ("...actually inconsistent."); the energy verifier detects the
    contradiction it was handed. `if 'inconsistent' in step_text` reproduces the
    identical 1.96x.
  exp3959 -- claimed 24.662x with the verifier NEVER IN THE LOOP: both arms are
    simulate_geometric() draws from assumed probabilities, object-vs-pixel search
    geometry, despite inference_substrate claiming "real games".
  exp3405 -- carries an adversarial-verify corrigendum recorded at commit time.

Clearing those would have re-admitted results a human review deliberately
quarantined. The gate changing its mind is not the same as the finding being
wrong.

So the defect is not the stale stamps. It is that a determination is stored as a
bare boolean with its reason somewhere no reader will look, while
conductor_gates.py keys off the field's presence. The refactor is to make each
determination self-describing:

  9 KEPT, each now carrying flagged_adversarial_reason. Three hold the recovered
    findings above; six record that the declared state is itself the reason --
    two `disqualified:` verdicts, a vivado_simulated_success, an auroc=0.0000
    capstone, a write_path_missing, and one artifact whose status is
    preconditions_recorded with no honest_verdict at all.
  34 CLEARED, each with flagged_adversarial_cleared_note beside a field set
    false and never removed.

Every artifact was re-verified INDIVIDUALLY at write time rather than on the
strength of the analysis table; a live CRITICAL would have aborted that artifact
and none did.

THE CORRECTION. The applier resolved an experiment id with
`sorted(glob("experiment_<id>_*.json"))[0]`, which picks alphabetically. Two ids
own several files, so each got the decision meant for a sibling: 1736 went to
_ebt_gradient_refinement instead of _kanele_synth, and 6275 to the
.formal_sidecar instead of the benchmark. The 1736 case is the serious
direction -- it ADDED a quarantine stamp to an artifact that had never carried
one and that no gate had ever flagged. A fabricated quarantine is the same
defect as a fabricated result, pointed the other way.

Caught by a cross-check of modified-versus-intended paths. That check had
already reported "zero strays" once, wrongly, because it re-derived the paths
with the same broken glob instead of comparing against the authored list -- a
verification that reproduces the bug it is checking for. An id is not a path in
a corpus with sidecars and same-numbered siblings.

Both wrong writes were corrected THROUGH determination_preservation_lint.py
rather than around it. The lint refused the obvious fix -- restoring the files
to field-absent -- and it was right to: it cannot tell a mistaken determination
from a real one, and that is the property that makes it useful. The erroneous
fields are set false with a note saying plainly they were never real
determinations, rather than deleted, because erasing them would erase the
evidence of the error.

Verified after writing: 43/43 artifacts carry the intended state, flagged_
adversarial absent from 0, 0 corrigendum fields lost, determination-preservation
-lint OK, and the modified path list matches the authored list exactly.

